 ## Overview
-Rasterization-based Voxelization converts a continuous triangle mesh into a discrete volumetric representation by projecting the mesh geometry onto a 3D grid using graphics pipeline primitives or CPU-side scanline algorithms [S1][S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of generating a voxelized surface (shell) or point cloud from polygonal data without performing explicit surface reconstruction or interior filling [S1][S2, S4, unofficial]. It differs from ray-tracing methods by leveraging rasterization coverage to determine occupancy, and from dense voxelization by often employing sparse hash-table data structures to store only occupied voxel coordinates, making it memory-efficient for sparse scenes [S3, S4, unofficial].
+Rasterization-based Voxelization converts a continuous triangle mesh into a discrete volumetric representation by projecting the mesh geometry onto a 3D grid using rasterization primitives [S1][S4, S5, unofficial]. It solves the problem of generating a voxelized surface (shell) or volume from polygonal data without performing explicit ray-triangle intersection tests for every voxel [S1][S5, unofficial]. It differs from [Voxel Carving](1213-voxel-carving.md) by using constructive rasterization rather than destructive projection, and from [Analytic and Algebraic Methods](7913-analytic-and-algebraic-methods.md) by relying on discrete sampling of rasterized fragments rather than solving geometric equations [S5, unofficial].
 
