 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Holes:** Standard rasterization only covers pixels/voxels whose centers lie inside the triangle, leading to gaps [S6, unofficial]. Mitigated by precision factors or conservative rasterization [S2, S6, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Output mesh size scales with voxel density. A torus (441 vertices, 800 faces) with voxel size 0.75 results in 61,312 vertices and 91,968 faces [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** Using dense 3D arrays is inefficient for sparse meshes; hash tables are preferred [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Holes:** Standard rasterization may leave gaps if triangle centers do not align with voxel centers. A precision parameter or conservative rasterization is required to fill these [S2, S6, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The output mesh size scales with voxel density. A torus with 800 faces can result in >140,000 vertices at small voxel sizes [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Sparse vs Dense:** Using a hash set avoids memory allocation for empty space but adds lookup overhead [S3, unofficial].
 
